<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</title><subtitle type="html">Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger</id><updated>2026-11-08T15:24:33Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-ger" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-1432?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>'Never Again!': Holocaust remembrance under pressure</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e1f89635f9f83b5d7cdb19f9812f06acb1656d7f3f77061387027faf067769f4</id><updated>2026-11-08T15:24:33Z</updated><published>2026-11-08T09:54:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/never-again-holocaust-remembrance-under-pressure/a-79840894--861f4756e8c3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/never-again-holocaust-remembrance-under-pressure/a-79840894?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Only a few Holocaust survivors remain who can share their stories firsthand. Meanwhile, voices calling for an end to what they describe as a "cult of guilt" are growing louder. How can Holocaust remembrance survive?</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Son of Iran's shah receives 'Freedom Prize' in Frankfurt</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2f454de2851a110205974f40355e128af6ec2f31a457137634d862222a75cd7b</id><updated>2026-11-08T15:23:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-07T14:38:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/son-of-iran-s-shah-receives-freedom-prize-in-frankfurt/a-79885661--aa99dccac573.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/son-of-iran-s-shah-receives-freedom-prize-in-frankfurt/a-79885661?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">18,000 people gathered in Frankfurt to show support for Reza Pahlavi, the son of the Iranian monarch ousted in 1979. Factions in the opposition have accused him of not distancing himself from his father's rule.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Ilse Aigner tipped by party allies as next German president</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4ce0535a581ba0ce86aabddb1136f5ba2609021f402f49f3caf4008be0a5c717</id><updated>2026-11-08T15:23:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-06T18:48:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ilse-aigner-tipped-by-party-allies-as-next-german-president/a-79880594--62497e57d523.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ilse-aigner-tipped-by-party-allies-as-next-german-president/a-79880594?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">German media reports suggest that Bavarian politician Ilse Aigner's chances of becoming the next president are on the up. Long favored by her Bavarian CSU, now it seems Christian Democrats nationwide plan to back her.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany: Small plane crashes onto A2 highway, killing 2</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c0b22df14d1aefb099a25002e68f76c377847b87359234ce48b273bd6a1f0963</id><updated>2026-11-08T15:23:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-06T16:01:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-small-plane-crashes-onto-a2-highway-killing-2/a-79878930--fda2d6a1e11f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-small-plane-crashes-onto-a2-highway-killing-2/a-79878930?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Two people have died after a small plane crashed onto Germany's A2 highway near Magdeburg. The crash closed the Hannover-bound lanes on a major east-west route, with no road vehicles reported to be involved.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany hopes to host world ice hockey championship — without Russia</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:636e0204ceb59d7ffb5b7b983455e1fb51d65ef19ea2fd2ef4e5c25841d167d6</id><updated>2026-11-08T15:23:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-07T11:06:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-hopes-to-host-world-ice-hockey-championship-without-russia/a-79879536--60661c53ebb2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-hopes-to-host-world-ice-hockey-championship-without-russia/a-79879536?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">International protests continue after a court ruled that Russia must be allowed to return to international ice hockey. Germany is seeking a solution that will allow the country to host next year's world championship.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Germany’s Gen Z protests spark debate on integration, bias</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c2b64639f327faa82a7d3a134ea3a01abf59b89f3177c7f3e9ce87297173c3d6</id><updated>2026-11-08T15:23:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-05T16:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-s-gen-z-protests-spark-debate-on-integration-bias/a-79868497--c2e5ae27f840.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-s-gen-z-protests-spark-debate-on-integration-bias/a-79868497?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Political debate about the recent Gen Z riots have raised the question: Can Germany talk about problems with integration without putting entire groups of people under suspicion?</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>The dachshund, a humorous icon with political symbolism</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fcad094f4a9f7f57f486e175adf4a7fddc27e16b2628019f32c6322923422652</id><updated>2026-11-08T15:23:27Z</updated><published>2026-10-30T07:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191--0ec0144d606e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">From Picasso to Andy Warhol, a surprising number of artists have portrayed the German breed. But the dachshund has also at times been co-opted as a political symbol.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry></feed>
